course: dictionary
lesson: 2365
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word cuff is a good example. Read this: «He met a rabbit cleaning his silly face with his paws, and had to cuff his head once or twice to get any sense out of him.» Cuff cannot mean a shirt sleeve here, because a sleeve cannot clean a face or be used to get sense out of someone. The clue is the word once or twice: cuff means hit lightly with an open paw.
explain: Now read this: «So the Otter cuffed him and left him.» Ask yourself what would fit. The sentence is about a fight or a telling-off, not clothes, so cuff must mean a light hit. Try the meaning in the sentence: «So the Otter hit him lightly and left him.» It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
explain: Cuff can also be a noun, the part of a sleeve around the wrist. The police may also cuff a suspect, which means put handcuffs on them. Each meaning comes from a different sentence, so always check the clues around the word before you choose.
fact: Many words have more than one meaning. Context tells you which meaning the writer means.
fact: A cuff is the end part of a sleeve, around the wrist.
fact: To cuff someone means to hit them lightly with an open hand, as in «had to cuff his head once or twice».
fact: In crime stories, to cuff someone means to put handcuffs on them.
fact: «Off the cuff» means said without preparing, and «on the cuff» (US) means on credit.
exercise: «He had to cuff his head once or twice to get any sense out of him.» What does cuff mean here? | Hug tightly | Wash with soap | Tie up with rope | *Hit lightly with an open paw
exercise: What is a cuff on a shirt? | *The end part of a sleeve, around the wrist | The collar at the neck | A button on the pocket | A band of cloth at the top of a sock
exercise: «The police will cuff the suspect.» What does cuff mean? | *Put handcuffs on | Ask a question | Let go | Give a reward
test: Which phrase means said without preparing? | *Off the cuff | On the cuff | Cuff of the sleeve | Cuff round the ear
test: A nurse wraps a cuff around your arm. What is it for? | To hold a plaster in place | To keep your arm warm | *To measure blood pressure | To count your heartbeats
exam: «So the Otter cuffed him and left him.» What does cuffed most likely mean? | Fed | *Hit lightly with an open hand | Carried away | Sang to
